course: adult-life
lesson: 26
title: What people leave behind
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: When someone dies, their love and memories stay, but so do their things: money, a house, a car, a ring, a drawer full of old photos.
explain: Grown-ups then have a long, tiring job: following the dead person's wishes, filling in forms, sorting the house and deciding who keeps what.
explain: Picture a Saturday in Leeds: Mum, Auntie Sue and you are in Nan's kitchen with cardboard boxes, wrapping her teacups in newspaper, laughing at her old hats and then suddenly crying over a birthday card she kept.
explain: Pets leave a gap too: when the vet helps a very old dog die gently, the family grieves and often holds a small funeral in the garden.
explain: All of this is sad but normal, and none of it is a child's job to fix.
fact: A will is a legal paper someone writes while alive, saying who gets their money, house and belongings after they die; the person who carries it out is called the executor.
fact: Probate is the official permission from a court to deal with a dead person's money and property, and until it comes the house usually cannot be sold; it can take months.
fact: Families sometimes argue over who gets what after a death, especially a house, because grief makes people touchy and old quarrels come back.
fact: Such family fights can last a long time and may involve lawyers, but they often calm down in the end, and a child never has to take a side.
fact: Money left to a child is usually kept in a savings account until they are eighteen, so it can help with something important later.
fact: Clearing out a house after a death means sorting the person's things to keep, give to family, sell or give away; it is slow, sad work because every object holds memories.
fact: A pet "put to sleep" was given a painless injection by the vet so it died peacefully, because it was very ill or very old; it will not wake up.
fact: Owners choose to have a pet put to sleep to stop it suffering, and it is one of the hardest decisions a family makes.
fact: Small pets are often buried in the garden, in a shoebox or wrapped in a cloth, with a stone or a cross on top, as a small funeral.
fact: A keepsake is something that belonged to someone who died, kept to feel close to them, such as a ring, a jumper or a recipe book.
fact: A keepsake can be very precious even when it is worth little money, so you should ask before touching it.
fact: Life insurance is something people pay for every month so that, if they die, their family gets a sum of money to help pay for the house and daily life.
fact: Having life insurance is a sensible plan made by many parents; it does not mean anyone expects to die soon.
exercise: Mum says "Grandma left a will." What is a will? | A paper saying who gets her things after she dies | A list of Grandma's favourite recipes | A promise to visit every Sunday | A letter Grandma wrote to her doctor
exercise: Who carries out what a will says? | The executor | The family vet | The undertaker's driver | The youngest grandchild
exercise: Dad says "We're waiting for probate to come through." What are they waiting for? | Official permission to deal with the dead person's money and house | A parcel of Grandad's clothes from the hospital | The funeral flowers to be delivered | A new doctor to sign a sick note so Dad can stay off work
exercise: Why can't the family sell Nan's house straight away? | They usually need probate first | Houses can never be sold after a death | The neighbours have to agree first | The house must stay empty for ten years
exercise: "Auntie and Mum aren't speaking because of the house." What is going on? | They are arguing about who gets the house after the death | They both lost their voices from crying | They are too busy painting the house | They are playing a game of who stays quiet longest
exercise: Why do families sometimes argue after someone dies? | Grief makes people touchy and old quarrels come back | Families always secretly hate each other | The law tells them to argue | The dead person left a note asking them to fight
exercise: If Mum and Auntie fall out, what should you do? | Keep loving both and stay out of the fight | Choose the side with the bigger house and stick with them | Tell everyone at school all about the big row | Refuse to talk to either of them until they make up
exercise: "Grandad left you a little something. It's in a savings account until you're eighteen." What does that mean? | Grandad left you money that is kept safe until you grow up | Grandad left a small toy that the bank is keeping in a locked box | You must pay Grandad's bills when you are eighteen | Grandad will send you pocket money every week until then
exercise: "We're clearing out Nan's house this weekend." What will the grown-ups do? | Sort Nan's things to keep, give away or sell | Clean the house so Nan can move back in | Knock the house down to build a new one | Have a big party for Nan's birthday
exercise: Why is clearing a house after a death so slow? | Every object brings back memories | The furniture is glued to the floor | The law allows only one box to go a day | Nobody else is allowed to help them
exercise: Mum, red-eyed: "We had to have Biscuit put to sleep." What happened to Biscuit? | The vet gave him a painless injection and he died | He is having a long nap at the vet's and will wake up later | He was given away to another family | He ran off and is sleeping somewhere else
exercise: Why would a family choose to have their pet put to sleep? | To stop a very ill or old pet from suffering | Because they got bored of looking after it and wanted a new one | Because pets must die at ten years old | Because the vet needed a new pet
exercise: Why do families bury a dead hamster in a shoebox at the bottom of the garden? | It is a small funeral and gives a place to remember | So the hamster can dig its way out later | Because the bin men do not take pets | To grow a new hamster next spring
exercise: Mum wears Nan's ring and says "It's to remember her by." What is the ring? | A keepsake | A prize | A loan | A spare
exercise: Why is a keepsake precious even if it is cheap? | It helps someone feel close to a person who died | It slowly turns into real gold after many years in a drawer | It is the only ring Mum is allowed to wear | Shops no longer sell rings like it
exercise: "Luckily he had life insurance." What does that mean for his family? | They will get money to help them live after his death | His death was covered by a doctor's guarantee, so it was not his fault | They will get him back after a few weeks | They do not have to go to the funeral
exercise: Your parents buy life insurance. What does that tell you? | They are planning sensibly for the family | They know from the doctor that they are going to die soon | They are secretly very ill | They want to make money from dying
test: Overheard on the phone: "The solicitor says nothing can be sold till probate's done, could be months." Why is Mum fed up? | The paperwork to deal with the dead person's property is slow | The solicitor secretly wants to buy the house cheaply for himself | The house has already been sold to strangers | Probate is a disease spreading in the family
test: Your brother says "When Grandma made a will she was planning to die soon." Put him right. | No, people write wills while healthy so things are sorted later | Yes, people only write a will in the very last week before they die | Yes, a will makes a person die sooner | No, wills are only written by doctors
test: Auntie on speakerphone, shouting: "She left the bloody house to YOU? After everything I did for her?" What is happening? | A family argument about what was left in the will | Auntie is pleased the house went to Mum | Auntie is talking about a house she wants to rent | Auntie has lost the keys to the house
test: Cousin Liam, 9, gets told the money from Grandad is "locked away till he's 18". Liam: "so it's gone then." Which is right? | No, it is saved for him and he gets it when he is grown up | Yes, the bank keeps it for ever | Yes, it was given to the government | No, he can spend it on sweets tomorrow
test: At Nan's house, Mum finds a box of letters Nan kept for sixty years and sits on the floor crying. Why? | Old things bring back strong memories of Nan | She has hurt her back lifting all the heavy boxes down the stairs | The letters are old bills Nan never got round to paying | She is crying only because the box is so heavy
test: Your little sister asks "Will Biscuit wake up after the vet made him go to sleep?" What is the honest answer? | No, Biscuit died peacefully and will not wake up | Yes, in a few days he will come home | Yes, when he has had enough rest he will come home to us | No, but the vet will give us Biscuit again
test: Dad, digging by the shed: "dig it deep enough or the fox'll have him." What is Dad doing? | Burying the family's dead pet in the garden | Planting potatoes for the winter | Hiding the family's money where burglars cannot find it | Digging a pond for frogs
test: Josh grabs Mum's old ring off the shelf to try on. Mum: "Put that down NOW." Why is she so sharp? | It belonged to someone who died and is precious to her | The ring is made of special glass and might break if it is dropped | Rings are against the rules for children | She was going to sell it tomorrow
test: Gossip at the school gate: "Poor thing lost her husband, but at least there was insurance, so they keep the house." What does the insurance do? | It pays money so the family can keep their home | It brings her husband back to life | It pays for a long holiday abroad so she can forget about it | It means the death was not real
test: Which of these is a keepsake? | Grandad's old watch kept in a drawer to remember him | A shiny new watch bought at the shops in town yesterday | A receipt from the supermarket | A borrowed library book
test: Mum, tired: "Probate, the will, the house, the bank, I'm sick of forms." What is the best way to help? | Be kind and give her a hug or a cup of tea | Ask her to fill in your school forms while she's at it | Tell her the forms are her own fault for not starting sooner | Hide the forms so she cannot find them later
exam: Identify the principal function of a will executed by a person during his or her lifetime. | To specify the disposal of the person's property after death | To appoint a physician for the person's final illness | To record the person's preferences in respect of meals and diet | To guarantee that the person will live a long and healthy life
exam: It is given that the sale of a deceased person's dwelling is deferred pending the grant of probate. Ascertain the nature of probate. | Official court permission to administer the deceased person's estate | A medical certificate confirming the cause and the time of the death | A religious ceremony held prior to burial | An agreement between neighbours concerning the property
exam: Select the response that most accurately characterises the expression "to have a pet put to sleep". | To have a veterinary surgeon end its life painlessly | To administer medicine so that it sleeps for a long period | To transfer it permanently to another household | To leave it overnight at a boarding kennel
exam: Determine what is thereby asserted when it is stated that a deceased person "had life insurance". | A sum of money becomes payable to the family upon the death | The family is legally required to pay the deceased person's debts twice | The death may be reversed upon application to the insurer | The family is excused from attending the funeral
